<spell-notebook "dimmer usage">
	<header level="1">
		Dimmer
		<header>A dimmer hides distractions to focus attention on particular content.</>
	</>
	<dimmer-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<html>
			<ui-menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
				<ui-item link="#using-it">Using it</>
				<ui-item link="#showing-and-hiding">Showing and hiding</>
				<ui-item link="#events">Events</>
				<ui-item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
				<ui-item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
				<ui-item link="#framework-usage">Framework usage</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  element vs page dimmer, `visible`, `closedby`, events, invokers, keyboard, accessibility, frameworks. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Put a `<ui-dimmer>` inside what it should cover; its children are the content, centred. Unlike Fomantic's, a
			dimmer is never created for you: write the element.

			- An ELEMENT dimmer (the default) covers its parent: the nearest positioned box, and the page stylesheet
			  positions a plain parent for it. Like Fomantic's, it is not modal: what it covers stays in the page.
			- A PAGE dimmer (`page`) is a `<dialog>` shown with `showModal()`, and it IS modal: it covers everything, so the
			  page behind it is inert (no Tab, no screen reader), focus moves inside and comes back when it hides, the page
			  can't scroll, and Escape closes it.
			- `show-on="hover"` / `show-on="click"` makes it show by itself, from its parent; otherwise only `visible` shows it.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Showing and hiding">
		<markdown>
			`visible` is the controlled state, as on every element (`hidden` is its other name): writing either shows or
			hides the dimmer and fires nothing (Fomantic's `.dimmer('show')` / `'hide'`). A dimmer starts hidden. The
			events' `detail` is `{ visible, originalEvent? }`, plus `reason` on `ui-closing`.

			`closedby` says what hides it, as on `<dialog closedby>`:

			- `any` (the default): a click on the dimmer (not its content), and Escape on a page dimmer
			- `closerequest`: Escape only
			- `none`: only `visible`

			A hover dimmer ignores clicks. Buttons drive it with no script: `commandfor` + `command="--show"`, `--close` or
			`--toggle`, as the examples do.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const busy = document.querySelector("#busy") // <ui-dimmer id="busy" page closedby="none" aria-label="Saving">
			busy.visible = true // show from code:  no event
			await save()
			busy.visible = false
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			User actions (the `show-on` trigger, a click, Escape, an invoker command) dispatch the cancelable `ui-opening` /
			`ui-closing` first (`detail.reason` is `click`, `escape`, `hover` or `close-all`), then `ui-opened` / `ui-closed`
			after the fade -- Fomantic's `onShow` / `onVisible`, `onHide` / `onHidden`.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const dimmer = document.querySelector("ui-dimmer")
			dimmer.addEventListener("ui-closing", (event) => {
			  if (event.detail.reason === "click" && unsaved) event.preventDefault() // stays shown
			})
			dimmer.addEventListener("ui-closed", () => console.log("hidden"))
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			- **Tab** moves focus inside a page dimmer only (the page behind is inert).
			- **Escape** closes a page dimmer (`closedby` `any` or `closerequest`).
			- A hover dimmer also shows when focus moves inside its parent.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- A page dimmer is modal: a `<dialog>` in the top layer, the rest of the page inert, focus inside (on the dialog
			  itself when nothing inside can take focus) and back where it was when it hides. Name it with `aria-label`;
			  otherwise it is "Dimmed page" (translated).
			- An element dimmer is NOT modal: what it covers can still be reached with Tab and read. If it means "busy", mark
			  the covered region `aria-busy` or `inert` yourself.
			- A hover dimmer also shows on keyboard focus inside its parent, and stays laid out (transparent) while hidden,
			  so Tab reaches its buttons. Fomantic's was mouse-only.
			- Escape closes a page dimmer unless `closedby="none"`; with `none`, hide it from code.
			- If the element fails to render, it dims nothing: it shows its children through a bare `<slot>`,
			  where they're written, and drops `visible`, covering and blocking the page, Escape, the `show-on`
			  triggers, the `ui-opening` / `ui-closing` vetoes and the fade.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Framework usage">
		<markdown>Bind `visible` to your state and listen for `ui-closing` to follow the user.</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-react">React 19</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			<ui-dimmer page visible={busy} aria-label="Saving" onui-close={() => setBusy(false)}>...</ui-dimmer>
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-vue">Vue 3.5</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<!-- compilerOptions.isCustomElement = (tag) => tag.startsWith("ui-") -->
			<ui-dimmer page :visible="busy" aria-label="Saving" @ui-closing="busy = false">...</ui-dimmer>
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-solid">Solid 2</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			// events and rich data are plain props:  `on-<event>={...}`, `options={...}`
			<ui-dimmer
			  page
			  visible={busy()}
			  aria-label="Saving"
			  on-ui-closing={() => setBusy(false)}
			>
			  ...
			</ui-dimmer>
			```
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
